Split the single interleaved loop into a read phase and a write phase:

```js
// BATCHED: one layout for all reads, one layout at end of frame
function equalizeHeights(cards) {
  // Phase 1 - read everything. Layout runs at most once, on the first read.
  const heights = cards.map(card => card.offsetHeight);

  // Phase 2 - write everything. Nothing reads, so nothing forces a flush.
  cards.forEach((card, i) => {
    card.style.height = heights[i] + 20 + 'px';
  });
}
```

Now the browser runs layout **once** during the read phase (or zero times if it was already clean), and **once** at the end of the frame after all writes. Two layouts instead of 199. The 400ms freeze becomes roughly 5ms.

Nothing about the algorithm changed — same reads, same writes, same result. Only the *ordering* changed, and the cost dropped by two orders of magnitude. That's what makes this such a satisfying fix, and why interviewers like it: it tests whether you understand the browser's execution model rather than whether you can micro-optimise JavaScript.

The rule generalises: **within one frame, do all your measuring before any of your mutating.**

`requestAnimationFrame` runs your callback **just before the browser paints the next frame**, which is the correct place for visual work. Used carelessly, though, it doesn't fix thrashing — it just relocates it.

The standard pattern separates the phases across the frame boundary:

```js
// Measure now, mutate in the next frame's callback
const rects = items.map(el => el.getBoundingClientRect());

requestAnimationFrame(() => {
  items.forEach((el, i) => {
    el.style.transform = `translateY(${rects[i].top}px)`;
  });
});
```

Three things worth knowing:

- **Never read layout inside a rAF callback that also writes.** rAF runs before layout for that frame, so a read there forces the layout you were about to get for free.
- **rAF is throttled in background tabs** (down to roughly once per second, or paused entirely), which makes it the right choice for animation and the wrong choice for work that must run regardless of visibility.
- **Libraries formalise this.** `fastdom` queues reads and writes into separate rAF-aligned phases so unrelated components can't thrash each other — genuinely useful when the interleaving is spread across a component tree rather than one function.

The failure that reaches production most often: a component that measures each row to decide something per-row — truncate the text, size a tooltip, decide whether to show a "read more" link.

```js
// Ships fine. Dies at scale.
rows.forEach(row => {
  if (row.scrollHeight > row.clientHeight) {   // READ
    row.classList.add('truncated');            // WRITE
  }
});
```

Why it survives review and then breaks:

- **In development the list has 10 items.** 10 forced layouts, ~15ms, invisible.
- **The demo account has 50.** Still under 100ms, feels slightly heavy, nobody files a bug.
- **The largest customer has 2,000.** ~2,000 forced layouts on a document that's now also much bigger, so each one costs more too. The cost is worse than linear, and the tab hangs for seconds.

That superlinear behaviour is the thing to internalise: layout cost grows with document size **and** the number of forced passes grows with list length, so the two multiply.

The fixes, in order: batch the reads; then question whether you need per-item measurement at all — `line-clamp` in CSS, `ResizeObserver`, or `IntersectionObserver` solve many of these cases with zero synchronous reads.
